# The bottom of a sum's descent. One row per reached layer that is not itself built from parts,
# with the route that reached it, so a leaf on two branches appears twice.
#
# Not the bottom of every descent
#   Which bottom applies depends on the quantity rather than on the graph. A sum over parts bottoms
#   out here, because below an F-terminal layer there is nothing left to add. A remainder bottoms
#   out earlier, at the first node whose own `n` and `d` were not both scaled by one factor with
#   width, because differencing them is legitimate there and the filed figure is a claim its
#   composer stands behind. composition/unsettled.sqlc is that predicate and
#   composition/settled_remainders.sqlc is that walk. A leaf satisfies it trivially, which is why
#   this relation looks like the general case and is not.
#
# A leaf is defined by what it is not
#   That is why this is an anti-join against a named relation rather than a `NOT EXISTS` inside a
#   predicate. composition/fusions.sqlc is "the layers that are assembled"; a reached layer missing
#   from it is a layer somebody measured directly. Naming the positive relation makes the negation
#   readable and gives the same set a second use elsewhere.
#
# More than one row per (root, leaf) is double supply
#   Count the rows per (root, leaf) and anything above one is the same supply entering one total
#   twice. Both routes are in the `path` column, which is what makes the finding actionable instead
#   of merely true.
#
# An anti-join that matches nothing looks like a working relation
#   It returns the whole left side, and nothing about the row count says the exclusion happened.
#   The law is recomputed rather than recorded, algebra/leaves.sqlc, every run. It is also the one
#   law with a live counter-example, below.
#
# `f.filing` is the right column to test
#   The choice is not free. It is the join key and `pm.part.composition` is NOT NULL, so a matched
#   row can never carry a NULL there. Testing a nullable column of the right-hand relation instead
#   would keep matched rows whose payload happens to be null: an anti-join that quietly
#   under-removes, with no symptom.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `leaves`.
#   Rewrite this file as `EXCEPT` and algebra/leaves.sqlc must fail, because descent is a bag on the
#   key and `EXCEPT` deduplicates rows away.
-- asrt:Part followed to a layer that names no parts of its own.
SELECT d.*
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) d
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
       ON f.filing = d.filing AND f.layer = d.layer
WHERE f.filing IS NULL
